WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.034 --> 00:00:01.325
- What do Rihanna

00:00:01.325 --> 00:00:04.063
and the Red Hot Chili
Peppers have in common?

00:00:04.063 --> 00:00:07.335
A deep admiration for Brandy Norwood.

00:00:07.335 --> 00:00:10.527
She's been called your favorite
singer's, favorite singer.

00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:12.895
The artist known as the "Vocal Bible",

00:00:12.895 --> 00:00:16.452
is both a Grammy winner
and television star.

00:00:16.452 --> 00:00:18.349
Brandy has had many highs in her career

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:21.789
and was the "It" girl in
the '90s and early 2000s.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:24.363
But even still, Brandy's vocal talent

00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:27.652
and innovation are not recognized enough.

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:28.702
So we are gonna talk about it.

00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:32.917
(people talking simultaneously)

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:34.450
Brandy was only 14 years old

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.453
when she recorded her first single,

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:37.286
"I Wanna Be Down".

00:00:37.286 --> 00:00:39.416
The minimal arrangement combined

00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:41.241
with her impressive vocal chops,

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:44.673
won over the hearts of R&amp;B
and pop listeners alike.

00:00:44.673 --> 00:00:49.123
("I Wanna Be Down" starting)

00:00:49.123 --> 00:00:51.803
What do you think made Brandy
stand out early in her career?

00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:53.642
What do you think made her pop?

00:00:53.642 --> 00:00:57.363
- So I think there is
this very polished look

00:00:57.363 --> 00:00:59.815
that was super intentional,
in this innocence,

00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:01.982
that made her very loving.

00:01:03.112 --> 00:01:06.015
So that worked with her tone.

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:07.986
Brandy, it's only one tone.

00:01:07.986 --> 00:01:09.971
- Well, you know it out
of a thousand voices.

00:01:09.971 --> 00:01:12.493
- She is just, she's a quiet storm.

00:01:12.493 --> 00:01:15.675
- But Brandy didn't reach that
level of success overnight.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:18.925
Born in 1979, she spent
most of her childhood

00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:20.684
in Carson, California.

00:01:20.684 --> 00:01:22.986
Brandy's father was a a gospel singer

00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:24.512
and a minister of music.

00:01:24.512 --> 00:01:26.066
- Yes, that's right.

00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:28.826
Brandy got her musical
foundation in the church.

00:01:28.826 --> 00:01:30.681
She even started singing
in the church choir

00:01:30.681 --> 00:01:32.129
at the age of two.

00:01:32.129 --> 00:01:32.978
Brandy's life was changed

00:01:32.978 --> 00:01:36.468
after seeing Whitney Houston
on TV for the first time.

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:37.881
She remembers telling her mom,

00:01:37.881 --> 00:01:40.539
"I wanna be as big as Whitney Houston."

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:42.216
By the time she was 12 years old,

00:01:42.216 --> 00:01:43.787
she was already singing backup

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:46.676
for the teen R&amp;B group, Immature.

00:01:46.676 --> 00:01:48.538
And it took her only a year or two

00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:51.305
to land her own record deal with Atlantic.

00:01:51.305 --> 00:01:53.746
- It's important to remember
that the musical landscape

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.319
of the 1980s looked nothing
like we're used to today.

00:01:57.319 --> 00:01:59.629
Labels struggled to
find mainstream success

00:01:59.629 --> 00:02:01.907
with young black women stars.

00:02:01.907 --> 00:02:03.919
There were a few artists
who let off their mark like

00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:07.708
Tracy Spencer, Penny
Ford, and Janet Jackson.

00:02:07.708 --> 00:02:09.518
But even they struggled to break out

00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:12.371
of the R&amp;B charts and cross over to pop.

00:02:12.371 --> 00:02:14.199
- Between white artists infusing pop

00:02:14.199 --> 00:02:17.137
with R&amp;B elements and black
artists getting closer

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:19.250
to mainstream than ever before,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.318
Brandy had a real shot
at becoming a superstar.

00:02:22.318 --> 00:02:25.548
And Brandy did become a superstar.

00:02:25.548 --> 00:02:28.160
In 1997, she starred in Cinderella,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.462
alongside her hero, Whitney Houston.

00:02:30.462 --> 00:02:33.931
She got her own TV show,
Moesha, sold Barbie dolls,

00:02:33.931 --> 00:02:37.080
and even cut a deal with CoverGirl.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.492
- So how did Brandi grow
into a heavy weight?

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.011
And what makes her voice so special?

00:02:42.011 --> 00:02:45.793
It all starts with her greatest
influence, Whitney Houston.

00:02:45.793 --> 00:02:48.321
You can hear the Whitney
influence in her debut album

00:02:48.321 --> 00:02:49.740
with songs like "Baby",

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.573
("Baby" starting)

00:02:54.594 --> 00:02:56.614
- or "Broken Hearted".

00:02:56.614 --> 00:03:01.104
("Broken Hearted" starting)

00:03:01.104 --> 00:03:04.183
- I feel like she was able
to use Whitney Houston

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:07.835
as kind of her launching pad
into being a lot more inventive

00:03:07.835 --> 00:03:09.845
and a lot more thoughtful and, okay,

00:03:09.845 --> 00:03:11.544
where else can I go, musically?

00:03:11.544 --> 00:03:12.505
- In what ways do you hear

00:03:12.505 --> 00:03:14.969
that Whitney influence in her music?

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:16.635
- You know, if you, if you really go back

00:03:16.635 --> 00:03:18.484
and you listen to a lot of her ballads,

00:03:18.484 --> 00:03:20.567
♪ Broken ♪

00:03:23.505 --> 00:03:24.593
All of that's Whitney.

00:03:24.593 --> 00:03:27.183
If you listen to that and
you put it side by side with

00:03:27.183 --> 00:03:29.862
♪ I believe in you and me ♪

00:03:29.862 --> 00:03:31.861
it's the same vocal style.

00:03:31.861 --> 00:03:34.285
- Yeah. And that, my
favorite one is the one

00:03:34.285 --> 00:03:35.803
it's the one that's like,

00:03:35.803 --> 00:03:40.803
♪ Let me live just so I can sing
for you, sounds like Brandy ♪

00:03:40.923 --> 00:03:44.064
- Yeah. You know, it sounds
like, if Whitney were still

00:03:44.064 --> 00:03:47.004
making music, something
that she would be doing.

00:03:47.004 --> 00:03:48.442
- Exactly.

00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:50.535
- [Male Narrator] Collaborating
with acclaimed musicians

00:03:50.535 --> 00:03:51.563
like Rodney Jerkins,

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:54.325
and late vocal producer, LaShawn Daniels,

00:03:54.325 --> 00:03:56.255
Brandy implemented a vocal style

00:03:56.255 --> 00:03:58.725
that felt effortless to the listener.

00:03:58.725 --> 00:04:02.709
("The Boy is Mine" starting)

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.191
- Brandy's vocal agility, fast vibrato,

00:04:05.191 --> 00:04:08.459
and crystal clear runs
soon became her trademark.

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:10.776
Rather than testing the
limits of her belting power

00:04:10.776 --> 00:04:12.145
and vocal range,

00:04:12.145 --> 00:04:16.389
Brandy focuses on her
mid-register with great control.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.306
("Long Distance" starting)

00:04:19.306 --> 00:04:22.031
- [Woman Narrator] Taking
inspiration from jazz singers,

00:04:22.031 --> 00:04:24.777
she often utilizes the
mixed voice approach,

00:04:24.777 --> 00:04:28.362
by mixing her chest
voice and her head voice.

00:04:28.362 --> 00:04:31.766
She brings an airy and buttery
quality to her singing.

00:04:31.766 --> 00:04:35.839
("Should I Go" starting)

00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:37.192
- [Male Narrator] Perhaps this is one of

00:04:37.192 --> 00:04:38.452
the reasons why her voice still sounds

00:04:38.452 --> 00:04:40.313
as flexible as ever today.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:43.511
Even when she belts,
she does so with care.

00:04:43.511 --> 00:04:46.038
She knows exactly how
long to sustain a note

00:04:46.038 --> 00:04:47.918
and how soon to descend off of it.

00:04:47.918 --> 00:04:52.538
♪ When you look at that
picture, la, so, la. ♪

00:04:52.538 --> 00:04:53.647
You know?

00:04:53.647 --> 00:04:55.147
- That's fire, that's fire.

00:04:55.147 --> 00:04:57.168
- Her use of tensions
and knowing how to like,

00:04:57.168 --> 00:05:01.019
use an approach note, you know,
and then like a suspension.

00:05:01.019 --> 00:05:04.859
So, and later years "Full Moon",
"Full Moon" she literally,

00:05:04.859 --> 00:05:07.526
(singing notes)

00:05:11.235 --> 00:05:13.076
While the chord is just
doing all the things!

00:05:13.076 --> 00:05:14.934
(narrator singing)

00:05:14.934 --> 00:05:17.881
(singing in unison)

00:05:17.881 --> 00:05:20.048
The woman is singing down.

00:05:21.131 --> 00:05:22.765
- Like many R&amp;B singers,

00:05:22.765 --> 00:05:25.516
Brandy is also a fan of adlibs.

00:05:25.516 --> 00:05:27.835
She often adds to the story of each song

00:05:27.835 --> 00:05:32.131
by inserting new melodic layers
to top off the main theme.

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:35.298
("Bordline" starting)

00:05:37.556 --> 00:05:39.555
- Every element feels intentional

00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:42.475
and it intensifies the emotional
impact on the listener.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.324
Grammy award winning
gospel singer songwriter,

00:05:44.324 --> 00:05:46.642
Erica Campbell said it best,

00:05:46.642 --> 00:05:49.575
"It's a perfect marriage
of vocal and lyric".

00:05:49.575 --> 00:05:52.929
- Brandy is piano, Brandy
is a string section.

00:05:52.929 --> 00:05:54.240
- Oh wow.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.927
She, her arrangements are arrangements.

00:05:56.927 --> 00:05:58.530
They're pads.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:05:59.979
- Orchestrations.

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:02.460
- Or an orchestration like, it's,

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.793
you could strip away all instrumentation

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.682
of a Brandy song and have a full song.

00:06:09.682 --> 00:06:11.650
- There's a reason why many
consider Brandy's third

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:14.059
album to be her greatest body of work.

00:06:14.059 --> 00:06:15.260
In "Full Moon",

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.338
it was time to showcase
the depth of her abilities.

00:06:18.338 --> 00:06:19.928
- Brandy said she believed the track,

00:06:19.928 --> 00:06:22.630
"What About Us" could change the game.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:24.882
The offbeat, futuristic
song took influence

00:06:24.882 --> 00:06:26.937
from hiphop and dance pop.

00:06:26.937 --> 00:06:30.520
("What About Us" starting)

00:06:31.692 --> 00:06:34.554
- Rodney Jerkins had a
field day on "Full Moon".

00:06:34.554 --> 00:06:36.881
And if you listen to "Full Moon",

00:06:36.881 --> 00:06:40.373
if you is the very first song
on there called, "I Thought",

00:06:40.373 --> 00:06:43.781
("I Thought" starting)

00:06:43.781 --> 00:06:48.064
you'll hear the way that he
used that same production

00:06:48.064 --> 00:06:51.675
on "Feedback" for Janet Jackson,
four or five years later.

00:06:51.675 --> 00:06:54.972
("Feedback" starting)

00:06:54.972 --> 00:06:59.085
- Rodney Jerkins
literally found his sound,

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:02.684
in my opinion, on the
Brandy "Full Moon" album.

00:07:02.684 --> 00:07:03.517
- Okay.

00:07:03.517 --> 00:07:05.915
- Brandy is an, is a singer that producers

00:07:05.915 --> 00:07:07.645
can find their sound in.

00:07:07.645 --> 00:07:09.006
- [Woman Narrator] As
she was in the studio

00:07:09.006 --> 00:07:10.945
finishing up the
production of "Full Moon",

00:07:10.945 --> 00:07:12.794
Brandy found out that she was pregnant

00:07:12.794 --> 00:07:14.793
with her daughter Sy'rai.

00:07:14.793 --> 00:07:16.200
- [Male Narrator] Now
the mother of a toddler

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.834
in her twenties, Brandy
was ready to show the world

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:21.582
what adulthood looked
like through her eyes.

00:07:21.582 --> 00:07:23.983
First, she said goodbye to
her longtime collaborator,

00:07:23.983 --> 00:07:26.755
Rodney Jerkins, and
brought in Timbaland to

00:07:26.755 --> 00:07:30.264
produce her fourth studio
album "Aphrodisiac".

00:07:30.264 --> 00:07:31.413
- [Woman Narrator] In this album,

00:07:31.413 --> 00:07:34.464
Brandy's voice sounds
more raw than ever before.

00:07:34.464 --> 00:07:36.733
Boldly referencing Coldplay in "I Try",

00:07:36.733 --> 00:07:39.633
this classic R&amp;B tune
aligns Brandy's adlibs

00:07:39.633 --> 00:07:41.842
with her building frustration and regret

00:07:41.842 --> 00:07:44.103
in the face of infidelity.

00:07:44.103 --> 00:07:47.186
("I Tried" starting)

00:07:48.921 --> 00:07:50.625
- With the release of "Aphrodisiac",

00:07:50.625 --> 00:07:53.562
Brandy's vocal trinity
was finally complete.

00:07:53.562 --> 00:07:58.003
Between "Never say Never",
"Full Moon" and "Aphrodisiac",

00:07:58.003 --> 00:08:00.435
she provided the perfect
guidebook for R&amp;B artists

00:08:00.435 --> 00:08:02.955
for many years to come.

00:08:02.955 --> 00:08:04.693
- Given the plethora of female artists

00:08:04.693 --> 00:08:06.522
now dominating the game,

00:08:06.522 --> 00:08:08.983
it's easy to forget how it all began.

00:08:08.983 --> 00:08:10.402
Solange once said,

00:08:10.402 --> 00:08:14.033
"Brandy is a goddess in
R&amp;B to a lot of people.

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:15.771
She is really the foundation for a lot

00:08:15.771 --> 00:08:19.733
of this very innovative,
progressive experimental R&amp;B.

00:08:19.733 --> 00:08:22.314
Brandy influenced a lot of that."

00:08:22.314 --> 00:08:23.890
- There's a reason why Solange,

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.771
one of the most experimental
R&amp;B artists of today,

00:08:26.771 --> 00:08:29.004
puts Brandy on a pedestal.

00:08:29.004 --> 00:08:31.953
Nowadays, it's common to
hear singers use their voices

00:08:31.953 --> 00:08:33.609
in unconventional ways,

00:08:33.609 --> 00:08:35.592
but back when Brandy was on her way up,

00:08:35.592 --> 00:08:37.833
that approach was practically unheard of.

00:08:37.833 --> 00:08:41.662
- Okay. So I have a question
that I'm nervous to ask you,

00:08:41.662 --> 00:08:44.741
but can you teach me some of
Brandy's vocal techniques?

00:08:44.741 --> 00:08:46.552
- Oh my gosh. Yes. What
song are we gonna do?

00:08:46.552 --> 00:08:47.472
(women screaming excitedly)

00:08:47.472 --> 00:08:48.392
- Oh! I dunno.

00:08:48.392 --> 00:08:50.523
- Okay, so my favorite song,

00:08:50.523 --> 00:08:53.672
one of my favorite songs
is, When You Touch Me".

00:08:53.672 --> 00:08:57.089
♪ Really miss you, baby. ♪

00:08:58.931 --> 00:09:00.002
- Okay.

00:09:00.002 --> 00:09:03.419
♪ Really miss you, baby. ♪

00:09:04.452 --> 00:09:06.650
- So you wanna be really specific

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.421
about where you put vibrato
when it comes to singing Brady.

00:09:09.421 --> 00:09:10.254
- Okay.

00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:11.842
Okay. It's gonna be challenge for me

00:09:11.842 --> 00:09:13.140
to not sing with vibrato.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.557
♪ Really miss you, baby. ♪

00:09:17.742 --> 00:09:19.591
Oh. I caught myself.

00:09:19.591 --> 00:09:21.432
- Vibrato isn't a bad thing.

00:09:21.432 --> 00:09:23.929
It's just not true to Brandy's aesthetic.

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:26.089
It's like, it's so specific in our world.

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:29.102
We call it "chiaroscuro",
the blend of light and dark.

00:09:29.102 --> 00:09:30.902
Like, it's just different for her.

00:09:30.902 --> 00:09:31.802
It's really unique.

00:09:31.802 --> 00:09:33.208
It doesn't really go by the standard.

00:09:33.208 --> 00:09:34.780
Like, you can tell she took the standard

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:38.019
and kind of have modified
it for her signature sound.

00:09:38.019 --> 00:09:40.420
- Today, Brandy is evidently still working

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:41.842
on expanding her range,

00:09:41.842 --> 00:09:45.210
showcasing a few previously
unheard falsetto tones

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.502
on songs like "I Am More".

00:09:47.502 --> 00:09:50.752
("I Am More" starting)

00:09:52.723 --> 00:09:55.762
- R&amp;B is a huge part of
mainstream pop music today.

00:09:55.762 --> 00:09:58.711
In part, thanks to Brandy
propelling the genre forward

00:09:58.711 --> 00:10:01.291
during a time where black
female artists were all

00:10:01.291 --> 00:10:03.251
but missing from the charts.

00:10:03.251 --> 00:10:05.840
Her TV shows, movies,
and endorsement deals

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:07.831
helped her reach role-model status,

00:10:07.831 --> 00:10:11.180
but it was her musical excellence
that cemented her legacy.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.360
Thanks for tuning in
to our Brandy episode.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:14.939
A friend of mine and the creator

00:10:14.939 --> 00:10:16.962
of "Sound Field" has
started in a new project

00:10:16.962 --> 00:10:19.760
inspired by "Sound Field"
that goes beyond music.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.499
I'll let Joseph tell you about it.

00:10:21.499 --> 00:10:23.089
- Working on "Sound Field" inspired me to

00:10:23.089 --> 00:10:26.648
create a new PBS documentary
series called "Subcultured",

00:10:26.648 --> 00:10:28.712
which explores how
lesser-known communities have

00:10:28.712 --> 00:10:31.840
influenced the mainstream
culture around us.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.631
I've met with a blind gamer

00:10:33.631 --> 00:10:35.500
who plays Mortal Kombat professionally.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:38.737
I've met cowboys and
cowgirls from the gay rodeo.

00:10:38.737 --> 00:10:40.780
And I've also explored how anime became

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.697
a $25 billion industry.

00:10:43.659 --> 00:10:46.498
- Check it out on the PBS
voices link in our description

00:10:46.498 --> 00:10:48.729
and let them know that
Sound Field sent you.

00:10:48.729 --> 00:10:50.991
- Here are your submissions
from our last video

00:10:50.991 --> 00:10:54.140
when we asked you what your
favorite pop-punk song is.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.549
- "Ever Fallen In Love" by
the Buzzcocks is perfect

00:10:56.549 --> 00:10:59.072
and I could look to it
every day until I die.

00:10:59.072 --> 00:11:01.577
- As always thanks for watching.

00:11:01.577 --> 00:11:05.350
We wanna hear what your
favorite Brandy vocal moment is.

